\section{Introduction}
Kantosi (ISO 639-3 xkt; Glottocode kant1249) is spoken in northern Ghana. Wikipedia classifies it as a Gur language and gives the alternate name Yare (Yarsi). Other names include Kantonsi and Dagaare-Dioula. Speaker estimates vary: 6,300 (Wikipedia, 2020), about 4,200 (Joshua Project) and 3,500 (Global Recordings). Search found very little educational material.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers, textbooks or school programmes for Kantosi were found in search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, ``Kantosi language'': overview and classification. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kantosi_language}
\item Glottolog reference record for Kropp Dakubu, Mary E. 1988, which appeared in the search results for Kantosi. We did not verify its content. \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/305475}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Joshua Project, Kantosi in Ghana: people-group profile. It states that Bible translation is needed and that audio Bible and Jesus Film versions are not available. \url{https://joshuaproject.net/people_groups/12502}
\item Joshua Project language page for xkt. \url{https://joshuaproject.net/languages/xkt}
\end{itemize}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Global Recordings Network, Kantosi (xkt): evangelism audio lessons are offered. \url{https://globalrecordings.net/en/language/xkt}
\end{itemize}

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
No evidence of schooling in Kantosi was found. Community-led orthography and materials development would be needed. Relatedness to neighbouring Gur languages of northern Ghana may help, but this was not examined in the search.
\end{document}
